From: RetroBPI_M2M <noreply@example.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_bcm: honour local-bd-address when no patchram
 file is loaded

The AP6212 (BCM43430A1) on the BPI-M2 Magic comes up with the ROM default
address AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA, even though the board DT supplies a valid
local-bd-address. Every pairing and every hid device is then reported
against that default:

  sony 0005:054C:0268.0001: BLUETOOTH HID v80.00 Joystick
      [Sony PLAYSTATION(R)3 Controller] on aa:aa:aa:aa:aa:aa

The cause is an ordering problem rather than a missing property. BT_HCIUART
is built in, so hci_bcm probes at ~0.97s, long before the rootfs is mounted
(brcmfmac only gets its firmware at ~5.0s). request_firmware() for the
patchram therefore fails:

  Bluetooth: hci0: BCM: firmware Patch file not found, tried:
  Bluetooth: hci0: BCM: 'brcm/BCM43430A1.hcd'

...even though that file is present on the rootfs. bcm_setup() then takes

	if (!fw_load_done)
		return 0;

and returns eleven lines above the code that would have made the DT
property matter:

	if (hci_test_quirk(hu->hdev, HCI_QUIRK_INVALID_BDADDR))
		hci_set_quirk(hu->hdev, HCI_QUIRK_USE_BDADDR_PROPERTY);

Without the patchram, btbcm_finalize() -- and with it btbcm_check_bdaddr(),
which is what sets HCI_QUIRK_INVALID_BDADDR -- never runs. So neither quirk
is ever set, hci_dev_init_sync() never calls
hci_dev_get_bd_addr_from_property(), and the DT address is silently ignored.

Set the quirk on the no-patchram path as well. The property is checked for
presence first: setting HCI_QUIRK_USE_BDADDR_PROPERTY with no property to
back it leaves public_addr as BDADDR_ANY, which keeps invalid_bdaddr true
and starts the controller HCI_UNCONFIGURED -- worse than a wrong address.

hdev->set_bdaddr is assigned at the top of bcm_setup(), before the early
return, so it is already in place for hci_dev_init_sync() to use. The
vendor Write_BD_ADDR (0xFC01) that btbcm_set_bdaddr() issues was verified
to work on this chip's unpatched ROM firmware by setting the address at
runtime over mgmt before writing this patch -- worth confirming, because a
failing set_bdaddr() makes hci_dev_init_sync() return non-zero and the
controller disappear entirely.

Signed-off-by: RetroBPI_M2M <noreply@example.com>
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
@@ -595,8 +595,25 @@
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
-	if (!fw_load_done)
+	if (!fw_load_done) {
+		/* No patchram file was found -- typically because the
+		 * controller probes before the rootfs holding it is mounted.
+		 * btbcm_finalize(), and with it btbcm_check_bdaddr(), will not
+		 * run, so HCI_QUIRK_INVALID_BDADDR is never set and the DT
+		 * property is never consulted: the controller silently keeps
+		 * its ROM default address.
+		 *
+		 * Honour local-bd-address here instead. Only do so when the
+		 * property is actually present -- setting the quirk without
+		 * one leaves public_addr empty, which starts the controller
+		 * unconfigured and unusable.
+		 */
+		if (hu->hdev->dev.parent &&
+		    device_property_present(hu->hdev->dev.parent,
+					    "local-bd-address"))
+			hci_set_quirk(hu->hdev, HCI_QUIRK_USE_BDADDR_PROPERTY);
 		return 0;
+	}
 
 	/* Init speed if any */
 	if (bcm->dev && bcm->dev->init_speed)
